                    {
                        "sense_id": "09875f71-7d7c-320a-aea5-5def0a1d1c03",
                        "entry_id": "e122a8c9-cf01-35ab-a8d7-2bedebcd1531",
                        "glosses": [
                            "of, or in, the jussive mood"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "933c7898-e8d3-4a5e-b872-fb46645b1fe6",
                                "sense_id": "09875f71-7d7c-320a-aea5-5def0a1d1c03",
                                "text": "De son étymologie même « joindre dessous », il induit quelque chose qui ressemble à un ordre. On parle alors d’un subjonctif jussif. Dans les phrases indépendantes ou principales, on rencontre souvent : qu’il vienne ! Qu’elle fasse cela avant mon retour ! On l’emploie aux personnes qui n’existent pas à l’impératif.",
                                "translation": "From its very etymology \"join underneath\", it indicates something that resembles an order. One speaks then of a jussive subjunctive. In independent or main sentences, we often find: \"Let him come!\", \"Let her do this before I return!\" We use it with persons that do not exist in the imperative.",
                                "english": "From its very etymology \"join underneath\", it indicates something that resembles an order. One speaks then of a jussive subjunctive. In independent or main sentences, we often find: \"Let him come!\", \"Let her do this before I return!\" We use it with persons that do not exist in the imperative."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
